<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide build defaults. The LINT gate (scripts/run-ci.sh) builds
    src/SignalWire with these on, so a warning is a build failure.

    Governing principle (cross-port, locked 2026-06-16): language idiom drives
    type/shape; every analyzer rule that is a genuinely useful standing signal
    is ON and burned to zero (burndown cost is not a disqualifier). A rule is
    turned OFF only when obeying it would change the SWAIG wire bytes or rename
    a wire token, or when it is genuinely low-value for this codebase. Per-rule
    severities + rationale live in .editorconfig.
  -->

  <!--
    SCOPE: EVERY project in this repo — the shipped library, the tests, the
    examples, the tools and the scripts' helper projects — carries the SAME
    analyzer bar. There is no looser tier for harness code (owner ruling
    2026-07-30: "all the full directories should be linted and formatted
    including tests examples and all ... examples and tests are shipping code
    too"). This PropertyGroup is deliberately UNCONDITIONAL: any condition
    scoping it to a subset of projects is the carve-out the ruling removes.
    Nothing in this tree is third-party vendored code, so nothing is excluded.
  -->
  <!--
    ANALYSIS LEVEL IS PINNED, NOT `latest`.

    `AnalysisLevel=latest` + `AnalysisMode=All` + `TreatWarningsAsErrors=true` is a
    SELF-UPDATING RULE SET: `latest` means "every rule this SDK knows about", `All`
    enables them at their default severity, and warnings-as-errors makes each one a
    build failure. So a new .NET SDK — a PATCH bump on a CI runner, no package
    release and no commit of ours involved — can enable new CA rules that instantly
    break the build. The LINT gate's verdict then depends on WHICH SDK the runner
    happened to install rather than on our source: red in CI on a commit that was
    green locally, with nothing in the diff to explain it and no local run able to
    reproduce it.

    This is the same hazard as an unpinned linter in every other port; .NET just
    ships the analyzers inside the SDK, which makes it easier to miss.

    Pinning the NUMBERED level (10.0) instead of `latest` means the rule SET is
    fixed by this file: a newer SDK still builds, but it does not silently switch on
    rules we have not burned down. global.json pins the SDK band itself as the
    second half of the guarantee (rollForward there keeps patch updates working).

    Raising this is a deliberate act: bump the number, build, and fix whatever new
    rules fire — that burndown is the POINT (per the governing principle above), and
    it happens when we choose rather than when Microsoft ships.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
    <!-- A lint violation fails the build → the LINT gate. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
